    "title": "Nevada DUI victim reacts to upcoming penalty changes - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Jezzamine Wolk Stephanie Overton ,\n4 hours ago\nLAS VEGAS ( KLAS ) — In Nevada, penalties for driving impaired and killing someone will increase in 2026. It comes after the Legislature passed a wide-ranging crime bill, and the governor signed it on Monday.\nCurrently, a DUI driver who kills can be sentenced anywhere from two to 20 years. Nevada law requires judges to sentence in ranges, where a felon is eligible for parole after serving 40 percent. The highest range is eight to 20 years.\nThe new law that goes into effect in January increases the maximum to 25 years. The most amount of time a DUI driver who kills could spend in prison, before seeing the parole board, becomes a decade.\n“Oh, it’s a step forward without a doubt,” said Sandy Heverly, executive director at STOP DUI.\nWhen asked if the penalty is enough, after four decades of fighting for safer roads, Heverly responded, “No.”\nHeverly’s ideal solution would be to change the current vehicular homicide law, removing the requirement that a driver already has three DUIs. A proposal to do that last legislative session failed. She said she wants to see the penalty for DUI with death rise to 25 years to life.\nHer family was driving to Disneyland when a drunk driver hit them more than 30 years ago. She, her husband, and their children faced serious injuries. Her mother was killed. Following the life-altering crash, she started STOP DUI to advocate for families like hers.\nAccording to Metro Traffic Lt. Bret Ficklin, DUI cases continue to climb in the Las Vegas valley. The holiday season is their busiest.\n“Times where people are having Christmas, Halloween, New Year’s togethers, that tends to be for us the deadliest time of the year,” Ficklin said.\nIn his last seven years on the job, Ficklin has seen the avoidable tragedy more times than he can count.\n“Too many people experience that, and we want to make sure that we don’t have any more people experience that,” he continued.\nHeverly and Fickland both shared a reminder to drive safely during the holidays at the Harry Reid International Airport annual tree lighting on Wednesday.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KLAS.\n4",
